nding stereotypes bias critical decision-making models the overall key principles are those our ethics values proportionality and the sanctity of human life and again these are all mandated through the state your police department has a commitment to training we mandate 16 hours of training a year that they have to go through that's mandated again they take an additional training as well and one thing we're really proud of with your your officers is to show you how dedicated they are since January of 2024 they have conducted over a hundred and 50 hours of training on their own time. They come in. They volunteer to come in. They work with training instructors before shift, middle of the day, on weekends. This is uncompensated time that they spend to make themselves better at doing their job, and we're really proud of that for the amount of work the officers put in. Again, like I stated, we do a lot of external trainings. CIT is probably one of the biggest ones and just be sure that your your police department is committed to getting better as I've said numerous times no one's perfect but I will say this you have one of the best police departments around and we're committed to training and getting better all right now I'll move on to I think what everyone's here for our use of force report and reporting procedures the police department likes to police officers may They only use force when it is objectively reasonable, proportional, and necessary given the totality of the circumstances. These circumstances include, but are not limited to, force necessary to effect an arrest, control an individual, lawfully detain a subject, prevent escape, or to protect themselves or others. We have a use of force policy that's been on our website since 2022. I would recommend everybody read it thoroughly. There's a lot of information in there. There's definitions. There's a lot on our website, and, again, it's been there since 2022. Officers will follow a use-of-force continuum, which